# Family A33: RabbitMQ publisher-confirm strategies
# (internal/rabbit/confirm). See hack/bakeoff.sh.
PKG=./internal/rabbit/confirm
BENCH=BenchmarkRabbitConfirm
REF=permessage
AUTO_KEY=confirm
KIND_DIM=""
# ADR-013 collects the §9 rows that need a broker and are therefore decided in
# Part B (the plan's "Phase-0 rows that need a broker (A11-A13, A15-A17,
# A32-A35) are decided in Part B (ADR-013)").
ADR=13
TITLE="RabbitMQ publisher-confirm strategy (A33)"
# The strategies live in the root module rather than in drivers/rabbitmq
# because hack/bakeoff.sh runs one `go test` in PKG from the repository root
# and a root-module `go test` cannot reach a nested driver module
# (and family A29's own failure). Nothing in the family needs
# a client library -- a confirm is four integers and a flag -- so the arms are
# measured exactly as the driver runs them, over the same Tracker.

#
# CRITERION is the benchmark configuration's decision column for this row, quoted.
# MATRIX is the IQMQ_BENCH_MATRIX grid the campaign measures the family at.
# DECIDED_AT names the benchmark-name segments the criterion is read at, and
# DECIDED_ON the metrics; `make bench-lint` lists the family's benchmarks at
# the declared grid and fails when a term or a metric is missing from the rows
# every kind has in common, which is the only data `bakeoff decide` reads.
CRITERION="throughput and ambiguity accuracy -> windowed/async for batch, per-message for Publish"
# MATRIX=default is the only grid: internal/rabbit/confirm has no
# IQMQ_BENCH_MATRIX branch, and its dimension is the batch width, which is the
# dimension §9.1's own expectation is stated in terms of.
MATRIX=default
DECIDED_AT="batch=1 batch=16 batch=128"
# virt-us/publish is "throughput" -- the publisher's elapsed time per message
# in the reference simulation's virtual microseconds -- and ambiguous/close is
# "ambiguity accuracy": how many publications a channel close leaves
# unresolved, which is the price the two pipelined kinds pay for the first
# number. stalls/publish is the mechanism behind the throughput and is reported
# so that a change in it can be attributed. ns/op is what the strategy's own
# code costs and is NOT the criterion: `OnSubmit` is one integer comparison in
# all three kinds, so a microbenchmark of the decision returns the same number
# three times.
DECIDED_ON="virt-us/publish ambiguous/close stalls/publish ns/op"
# What the simulation cannot measure is the broker's own service rate. Its cost
# model is one virtual microsecond of local work against a one-millisecond
# round trip, which is the ratio a channel to a broker on the same network
# shows and the ratio that makes the three kinds separable; a broker that is
# itself the bottleneck would compress the difference, and the absolute figures
# are virtual microseconds rather than nanoseconds of wall clock. The ratio
# between the arms is what is read. The correctness half of the row -- that a
# cumulative confirm resolves exactly the right publications, that a
# `basic.return` makes an ack a rejection, and that a channel close is
# Ambiguous rather than failed -- is TestModel and FuzzRabbitConfirm in the
# correctness gate, not a metric.
CRITERION_GAP="the broker service rate=./internal/rabbit/confirm sim_test.go fixes a virtual round trip so only the ratio between the arms is read -- wall-clock throughput lives in ./drivers/rabbitmq BenchmarkRabbitConfirm against a container"
# The family's decision rule is `rules["A33"]` in
# benchmarks/cmd/bakeoff/rules.go. §9.1 names TWO defaults -- "windowed/async
# for batch, per-message for `Publish`" -- and `AutoKind` can only be one, so
# the rule fixes the incumbent at permessage and records the other half as a
# per-call choice: `mq.Sink.Publish` has to return an outcome, which only a
# blocking strategy can do, and a batch or an `mq.AsyncSink` publication is
# where the window earns its ambiguity.
